1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:04,970
The brush reveals a string of beads, red as the setting sun.

2
00:00:04,970 --> 00:00:12,769
They are carnelian, a stone that does not come from the city where they were found. Somebody carried it, drilled it and sold it.

3
00:00:12,769 --> 00:00:16,377
Who traded these beads, and where did they go?

4
00:00:29,817 --> 00:00:38,587
How do we know? Archaeologists dug up workshops, beads, copper tools and weights. Seals found abroad also help.

5
00:00:38,587 --> 00:00:48,139
Harappan seals have turned up in Mesopotamia, and Mesopotamian texts name a distant land, Meluhha, which many scholars link to the Indus region.

6
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:08,609
Start with the fields. Harappan farmers grew wheat and barley, peas and sesame. They were among the first to grow cotton.

7
00:01:08,609 --> 00:01:16,217
Rice is known from Lothal. They kept cattle, buffalo, sheep and goats. Now the workshops.

8
00:01:16,217 --> 00:01:24,588
Potters used the wheel, and painted red pots with black designs. Craftsmen made bronze, which is copper mixed with tin.

9
00:01:24,588 --> 00:01:31,966
They made beads of carnelian, and seals of steatite. There is no iron. Trade was the heart of the cities.

10
00:01:31,966 --> 00:01:40,493
Goods moved by bullock cart, and by boat. Copper came from Rajasthan. Lapis lazuli came from Afghanistan.

11
00:01:40,493 --> 00:01:49,949
Seashells came from the coast. Goods also went overseas. Mesopotamian records speak of Dilmun, Magan and Meluhha.

12
00:01:49,949 --> 00:01:59,321
Dilmun is usually linked with Bahrain, and Magan with Oman. Meluhha is usually linked with the Indus region. Trade needs fairness.

13
00:01:59,321 --> 00:02:06,197
The Harappans used standard weights, cubes of chert stone, in neat ratios. And they wrote.

14
00:02:06,197 --> 00:02:15,141
The script has about four hundred signs, on seals and pots. The lines are short. Reading them is still an unsolved puzzle.

15
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:31,383
Scholars disagree. The Indus script has not been read.

16
00:02:31,383 --> 00:02:36,796
One view says the signs record a language, and many guesses have been made about which one.

17
00:02:36,796 --> 00:02:43,219
Another view says the signs are too short to be a language, and may only mark names, goods or status.

18
00:02:43,219 --> 00:02:45,870
Neither view has been proved.

19
00:02:57,783 --> 00:03:09,032
Carve this tip into memory. Wheat, wheels, weights, writing:. four W marks of Harappan trade. Fields, workshops, scales and signs.

20
00:03:09,032 --> 00:03:13,277
Four ideas that cover the economy of a Harappan city.

21
00:03:25,217 --> 00:03:38,590
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Name this red stone bead, made in workshops at Lothal and Chanhudaro.

22
00:03:38,590 --> 00:03:55,204
The answer: carnelian. Number two. Mesopotamian texts call the Indus region by what name? The answer: Meluhha. Number three.

23
00:03:55,204 --> 00:04:07,828
Harappan pots are red. What colour were the painted designs? The answer: black. Now one line for Mains.

24
00:04:07,828 --> 00:04:11,981
Examine the trade links of the Harappan civilisation.

25
00:04:27,917 --> 00:04:37,440
The beads go into their case, with tiny holes drilled by skilled hands. Three facts to take away. Carnelian, drilled and polished.

26
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:45,934
Made at Lothal and Chanhudaro. Traded as far as Mesopotamia. Next gallery: The Fading of the Indus.

